urn them into Universal gender neutral  washrooms but when they scan the slab to  start coring for the toilets they found  electrical conduits in the slab that  weren't on the asbo drawings now a  really big part of an architect's job is  coordinating between all the different  parties involved in construction and  with your consultant so first I'll go to  our electrical consultant and I'll ask  him is it okay if we core into this and  in this case he was like yeah it's  potentially an important conduit but it  will be impossible to know what it leads  to unless we actually trace the conduit  in the slab back to the source and of  course in a perfect world with an  infinite amount of time and money we  would probably do that it but the  library they need to open at a certain  time so that they can serve the public  so then you have a chat with a  contractor is this feasible  realistically how long do you think this  is going to take and So after talking to  them you might be like okay maybe a  better solution is just to go with long  toilets instead of floor mounted but if  we do that we're gonna need a much  bigger Plumbing wall here and now it's  an architectural issue these stalls are  getting really really tight so you could  move this wall out a little bit which is  going to eat into the holes area and so  you go and ask the library what do you  think about this and in this case the  answer was no because they'd be losing  an entire bookshelf and so now you go to  your last resort which is to move this  wall six inches down  so you're not going to get your five  foot turning radius here but you know by  code that's actually okay because this  over here is the only accessible stall  so anyways your job as an architect is  to talk to all of the parties involved  with the building code and accessibility  in mind and try to come up with the best  possible solution after everything's  been considered when you're in  construction everything is gonna seem  super super